  "solution": {
    "approach": "The task is straightforward: for each input string we need its last character. After reading T, we read each line as a whole string (including possible spaces). If the string is non‑empty, the character at index length‑1 is the answer. This is O(|S|) per test case for reading, and O(1) for extracting the character. The total complexity is linear in the total input size. Memory usage is O(1) besides the input buffer.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(total length of all strings) overall, O(|S|) per test case.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) extra space (output buffer only).",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        int T = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int t = 0; t < T; t++) {\n            String s = br.readLine();\n            if (s == null) s = \"\";\n            if (!s.isEmpty()) {\n                sb.append(s.charAt(s.length() - 1));\n            }\n            sb.append('\\n');\n        }\n        System.out.print(sb.toString());\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
